  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 140 AM Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A few isolated showers or storms are possible in \nthe south and west today and early Sunday.\n\nWeak high pressure will build in later today and tonight into Sunday \nfrom the northeast. A cold front presently across south-central \nVirginia will move through this morning and settle into South \nCarolina this evening and Sunday. To the west of us, a warm front \nwill set up across the Mid MS and western OH valley region in \nresponse to low pressure development across the Midwest.\n\nMorning to early afternoon stratus will gradually give way to \nsunshine and highs in the low to mid 80s. Forecast models indicate a \ngradual lowering of dewpoints from the north and east behind the \nfront and within northeast flow. However, lingering low to mid 70s \ndewpoints are expected in the southern Piedmont, Sandhills, and \nsouthern Coastal Plain. Instability of 1000-1500 J/kg may emerge \nearly this afternoon to promote a few isolated storms across our \nsouth and southwest along the southward moving front and sea-breeze. \n\nA few of the HREF members, specifically the HRRR/NAM-NEST, indicate \na decaying storm complex tied to the warm front tracking from the OH \nvalley into the northwest Piedmont and Triad early Sunday morning. \nInstability is expected to be weak, if at all present, thus limiting \nits eastward progression. Confidence on this scenario is low, but \ncertainly cannot rule it out. Any showers would be minor at this \nstage. Otherwise, morning stratus is possible over central NC Sunday \nmorning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected on Monday and Tuesday. Locally excessive rainfall will be \npossible. \n\nAloft, a nrn stream s/w low will track ewd across the nrn Plains and \nupper MS Valley Mon and Tue, amplifying the trough to the west. At \nthe surface, a backdoor cold front will approach from the north on \nMon, eventually making its way across the area Mon night/Tue as high \npressure ridges in. An area of low pressure may lift newd along/off \nthe Southeast and Carolina coasts through Tue night, increasing the \npressure gradient across the region. GFS forecast soundings show \nPWAT values of 1.7-2.0\" across the area, with SBCAPE of 500-1200 \nJ/Kg and 0-6 bulk shear of about 10 kts. Depending on the timing of \nthe front, there could be some lingering instability early Tue in \nareas south of it. Showers and thunderstorms are expected Mon and \nTue, with the potential for locally excessive rainfall. The WPC \nstill has all of central NC in a Marginal Risk (level 1 of 4) for \nexcessive rainfall both days, with a Slight Risk still clipping the \nnrn Coastal Plain on Tue. Highs Mon should still be well above \nnormal, mid 80s to around 90 degrees, decreasing to near normal for \nTue in the wake of the front.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... A CAD pattern looks to set up from mid to late next \nweek, resulting in mostly cloudy skies, patchy light rain and \ndrizzle, and below-normal high temperatures.\n\nMid/upper troughing will strengthen and build into the Eastern US \nfrom Wednesday through Friday. Meanwhile, anomalously strong surface \nhigh pressure will nose down from SE Canada and northern New England \ninto the lee side of the Appalachians, as a surface low drifts north \nin the western Atlantic, though models differ substantially on its \nplacement. This will result in robust N/NE low-level flow and a \nlikely CAD pattern across the southern Mid-Atlantic. Current \nforecast highs are in the upper-60s to upper-70s, but some raw \nguidance such as the GFS is even cooler. Forecast lows are generally \nmid-50s to mid-60s. POPs are in the slight to low chance range, and \ngiven the stable CAD regime, precipitation is more likely to be of \nthe stratiform variety (patchy light rain and drizzle) as opposed to \nconvective.",
